<TopicPage slug="subjects/statistics/expert/causal-inference">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Causal inference is the statistics of <strong>"what happens if we intervene"</strong> — the
    difference between "smokers get cancer more" and "smoking <em>causes</em> cancer". For most
    of the 20th century, statistics officially refused the question ("no causation without
    manipulation"). Since the 1980s–2000s (Rubin's potential outcomes, Pearl's do-calculus), it
    has a rigorous language — and it changed how medicine, economics and tech policy make claims.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four ideas that turn association into (defensible) causation" widget="equationStep" config={causalCfg} />

  <h2>The toolbox, in practice</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Potential outcomes:</strong> every person has two unseen outcomes (treated, untreated); causal effect = their difference. We see only one — inference is the art of estimating the missing counterfactual.</li>
    <li><strong>Confounders, mediators, colliders:</strong> control for the first, not the second, and never the third. Drawing the diagram first is what stops the classic "controlling away the effect" mistake.</li>
    <li><strong>Difference-in-differences:</strong> compare changes before/after between affected and unaffected groups — how economists evaluate minimum-wage and policy changes with no experiments.</li>
    <li><strong>Instrumental variables:</strong> a variable that nudges treatment but has no direct effect on outcome isolates the causal slice — the draft lottery did this for the question "does military service raise earnings?"</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Pearl, Causality (2009) and The Book of Why (2018); Imbens & Rubin, Causal Inference (2015); Nobel Prize 2021 citations.</p>
</TopicPage>
